To add support for a test listener that records the duration and final status of every test case in the googletest framework, you'll need to create a custom listener by subclassing `testing::TestEventListener`. Here’s a step-by-step guide on how to implement this:

### Step 1: Subclassing `TestEventListener`

You can create a custom listener by subclassing `testing::TestEventListener`. You'll typically want to override methods that trigger on the start and end of tests, such as `OnTestStart` and `OnTestEnd`.

### Step 2: Implementing the Listener

Here's an example implementation:

```cpp
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>

class DurationListener : public testing::TestEventListener {
public:
    explicit DurationListener(testing::TestEventListener* listener)
        : testing::TestEventListener(listener) {}

    void OnTestStart(const testing::TestInfo& test_info) override {
        // Record the start time.
        start_time_ = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        TestEventListener::OnTestStart(test_info);
    }

    void OnTestEnd(const testing::TestInfo& test_info) override {
        // Calculate duration and retrieve test status.
        auto end_time = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        std::chrono::duration<double> duration = end_time - start_time_;
        std::string status = test_info.result()->passed() ? "PASSED" : "FAILED";

        // Print or log the duration and status.
        std::cout << "Test " << test_info.test_case_name() << "." 
                  << test_info.name() << " Duration: " 
                  << duration.count() << " seconds, Status: " 
                  << status << std::endl;

        TestEventListener::OnTestEnd(test_info);
    }

private:
    std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::time_point start_time_;
};
```

### Step 3: Registering the Listener

To use your custom listener, you'll need to register it when you initialize googletest, typically in the `main` function:

```cpp
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    ::testing::InitGoogleTest(&argc, argv);

    // Add the custom listener to the default listener.
    testing::UnitTest::GetInstance()->listeners().Append(
        new DurationListener(testing::UnitTest::GetInstance()->listeners().Release()));

    return RUN_ALL_TESTS();
}
```

### Step 4: Compile and Run

Compile your code and run your tests. The custom listener will output the duration and status of each test case after it completes. 

### Conclusion

By following these steps, you can create a test listener in googletest that records the execution duration and final status of each test case. This allows you to track the performance and health of your test cases more effectively.

